Let me look at the actual vulnerable code more carefully. The issue is in `xmlParseElement` at line 9962-9963. Let me understand the exact scenario:

```c
void
xmlParseElement(xmlParserCtxtPtr ctxt) {
    if (xmlParseElementStart(ctxt) != 0)
        return;

    xmlParseContentInternal(ctxt);
    if (ctxt->instate == XML_PARSER_EOF)
        return;

    if (CUR == 0) {
        const xmlChar *name = ctxt->nameTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1];
        int line = ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1].line;
        ...
    }

    xmlParseElementEnd(ctxt);
}
```

The issue is that `ctxt->pushTab` is accessed here. `pushTab` is only allocated in `nameNsPush` when `sax2` is true. If `sax2` is false, `pushTab` is NULL, and `ctxt->pushTab[ctxt->nameNr - 1]` is a NULL pointer dereference.

But wait - the summary says "bad-cast". Let me look at this from the UBSan perspective. The `pushTab` is an array of `xmlStartTag` structs. When `sax2` is false, `namePush` is used, which only pushes to `nameTab` but not `pushTab`. So `pushTab` stays NULL.

